Design for per-source validation:

```ts
interface StoredTool {
  definitionSource: ToolDefinitionSource;
  execute: ToolExecutor;
  /** Set for a static source: the schema compiled once at register time. */
  staticValidate: ValidateFunction | null;
  /** Function sources compile per resolved schema, cached by schema object. */
  dynamicValidate: (schema: Record<string, unknown>) => ValidateFunction;
}
```

Simpler: one class `SchemaValidators`:

```ts
class SchemaValidators {
  private readonly bySchema = new WeakMap<object, ValidateFunction>();

  constructor(private readonly toolName: string) {}

  forSchema(schema: Record<string, unknown>): ValidateFunction {
    const cached = this.bySchema.get(schema);
    if (cached) return cached;
    const compiled = compileSchema(this.toolName, schema);
    this.bySchema.set(schema, compiled);
    return compiled;
  }
}
```

And register:

```ts
register(definition: ToolDefinitionSource, execute: ToolExecutor): void {
  const resolved = this.resolveDefinition(definition);
  const validators = new SchemaValidators(resolved.name);
  const staticValidate = typeof definition === 'function' ? null : validators.forSchema(resolved.inputSchema);
  this.tools.set(resolved.name, {definitionSource: definition, execute, validators, staticValidate});
}
```

Wait — for a static source, compiling at register via validators.forSchema also seeds the WeakMap keyed by the schema object; execute can then just call validators.forSchema(resolved.inputSchema) — the static schema object is stable (same reference every resolve), so the WeakMap hit = "compiled once". For a function source returning the SAME object each call → also cached ✓; returning a fresh object per call → recompiles per call (per spec: "caching compiled validators by schema object reference").

So no need to distinguish static/dynamic in StoredTool at all! `validators.forSchema(schema)` handles both: static sources resolve to the same object → one compile; function sources → per-object caching. And register compiles immediately for the resolved definition (fails at startup ✓). Elegant — and the register-time compile IS the seed.

